:topic:history: <br>
= aristotelian logic =
====== short ======
A brief summary of the chief features of defects of Aristotelian logic with an
eye to explaining the ways in which Fregean logic surpassed it.

====== related to ======
+++[[fregean_logic]]

== features ==
=== variables ===
Aristotle is the earliest scholars to attempt to codify the patterns of valid
arguments.
One of the major advances in Aristotelian logic was its use of variables. This
allowed regular patterns in the truth values between sentences realising various
forms to be explicitly described. Sentences of the form “Every A is B” are
universal affirmatives (A, in the scholastic square of opposition). The argument
“Socrates is a man; Every man is mortal; ∴ Socrates is mortal” is an instance of
the (valid) Barbara syllogistic form. Aristotelian logic didn't merely declare
that this particular argument was valid, but that any argument of this form was
valid.

=== categorical statements === 
Categorical statements are those that can be "analysed" into the following
components:
	a) a quantifier (‘every’, or ‘some’)
	b) a subject term
	c) a copula (‘is’ and its various tensed and modal forms)
	d) an optional negation (‘not’)
	e) a predicate term

For example: “Every man is mortal.”
But also variants (which Aristotle used more regularly) such as
	“Mortal belongs to every man.” and “Mortal is predicated of every man.”
are also categorical statements.

Aristotelian logic codified the possible combinations of truth values that
statements of this form might have. “Every smartphone is password-protected” and 
“No smartphone is password-protected” for instance, are contraries and cannot
both be true (but can both be false).

==== key difference ====
The same term (“man”, “animal”, “mortal”) may occur in categorical statements as
either subject or predicate.

Frege distinguished between concept and object so zealously that he
claimed that the concept horse was not a concept.
(see [[fregean_semantics#concepts are not objects]])
(see [[fregean_logic#predicates vs subjects]], [[fregean_logic#is]])

=== categorematic and syncategorematic terms ===
*Categorematic* terms (categorema/κατηγόρημα) such as “human”, “red” and
“Hypatia” are those that can be used as predicates or subjects.  Aristotle
claimed that general categorematic terms signified “some quality, or quantity or
relation, or something of that sort”(([[local:zz.texts/aristotle-completeWorks.oxfordRevisedTrans.pdf]])), while names of course signified the
object they referred to. (Names can be treated as signifying categories with
just one member - so “Socrates is mortal” was generally treated as “All
Socrates are mortal”.) These qualities/objects were the meanings of the terms.

Platonists held that general terms named a common nature or universal that was
distinct from its instances and yet wholly present in each them. While
nominalists might hold that these terms were connected to the categories of
objects of which they could be truly predicated via intermediate mental entities
such as concepts, or by linguistic convention. But in any case their meanings
fixed the category of object of which they were true and signified what those
objects had in common.

*Syncategorematic* terms (syncategorema/κατηγόρημα) such as “all”, “none”, “no”,
“not”, could not be used as either predicates or subjects,
and were viewed as modifying the meanings of other terms, but lacking meaning in
their own right. There was no thing they denoted, neither concrete objects (as
names and definite descriptions did) nor qualities or categories (as
categorematic terms did)

In later philosophers the distinction between categorematic and syncategorematic
terms would be developed into a distinction between terms that do or do not
“have meaning in isolation”
(see [[contextual_definition]])



=== unique form ===
Each categorical statement has just one analysis. Each is an A (Universal
affirmative – All _T_ are _U_)
or E (No _T_ are _U_) or I (Some T are U) or O (Some _T_ are not _U_ - often
weakened to “Not every T is U” in order to avoid existential import and avoid
problems arising from terms true of no objects).

It features in the constellation of inferences 
What would, with the benefit of hindsight be recognised as flaws, the inability
to 

The contrariety between 

Some categorical statements may occur in different syllogistic schema and in
different positions within those schema, but it has just one form and that form
exhaustively fixes the valid syllogisms in which it may occur.

In Frege's logic, by contrast, the same sentence could be a instance of multiple
different schema, and its realising each of those schema could give rise to
different inferential connections.

== defects on its own terms ==
Even on its own terms Aristotelian logic had notable shortcomings.

=== existential import ===
Aristotle simply hadn't considered categories which signified properties that no
object possessed.  The laws of Aristotelian/Scholastic logic required that
matching statements of the forms “Some S is P” and “Some S is not P” could not
both be false, and that if “Every S is P” was true then so too was “Some S is
P”. The orthodox way of achieving this was to treat “Every S is P” as false and
“Some S is not P” as true when there were no Ss.  The former, but not the
latter, was said to carry “existential import” and require the existence of
Ss for its truth.

Although this solution was never entirely satisfactory (apart from its unhappy
divergence from ordinary usage it obscured the difference between
“Some S is not P” and “Not every S is P”) the principles which created this
problem were fundamental to Aristotelian logic and essential to the symmetry of
its inferential laws.  In the absence of a better alternative, scholars
variously dismissed, delineated, attempted to resolve or simply lived with these
problems. The lingering effects of this disorder led Meinong (and Russell in
his earliest days) to posit a realm of merely subsisting objects in addition those
enjoying full-bodied existence.


